WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.577
Cloud GPUs exchange hardware ownership for flexible access to remote acceleration.

00:00:04.577 --> 00:00:08.647
They are especially useful for temporary demand, experiments, burst capacity,

00:00:08.647 --> 00:00:13.531
shared team environments, and hardware configurations that would be difficult to purchase.

00:00:13.531 --> 00:00:19.229
Local hardware may remain simpler or less expensive for steady workloads, sensitive local data,

00:00:19.229 --> 00:00:23.299
and users who need guaranteed access to a known system.

00:00:23.299 --> 00:00:26.148
Select an instance as a complete platform.

00:00:26.148 --> 00:00:31.846
The GPU model and VRAM must fit the workload, while virtual CPUs, RAM, storage,

00:00:31.846 --> 00:00:33.474
network capacity, region, quota,

00:00:33.474 --> 00:00:37.544
and actual availability affect whether the system works in practice.

00:00:37.544 --> 00:00:41.614
Calculate full-lifecycle cost, including idle time, disks, snapshots, object storage,

00:00:41.614 --> 00:00:44.056
data transfer, addresses, and operational labor.

00:00:44.056 --> 00:00:49.347
Use commitments only for predictable demand and interruption discounts only for restartable work.

00:00:49.347 --> 00:00:51.382
Make the software stack reproducible.

00:00:51.382 --> 00:00:55.045
Pin images and packages, verify acceleration, protect management interfaces,

00:00:55.045 --> 00:00:58.708
and test inference with representative prompts, contexts, and concurrency.

00:00:58.708 --> 00:01:00.743
Measure latency and throughput separately.

00:01:00.743 --> 00:01:05.627
Treat autoscaling as an operational system with startup delay and spending limits,

00:01:05.627 --> 00:01:09.290
not as an automatic cure for poor capacity planning.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.546
Finally, protect access and data throughout the lifecycle.

00:01:12.546 --> 00:01:16.209
Apply least-privilege IAM, private networking, authenticated APIs, secrets management,

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:19.058
encryption, retention rules, monitoring, and incident procedures.

00:01:19.058 --> 00:01:23.128
Stop or delete compute when it is no longer needed,

00:01:23.128 --> 00:01:26.791
then account for storage and network artifacts that remain.

00:01:26.791 --> 00:01:32.082
Successful cloud GPU use means renting the right capacity for a defined job,

00:01:32.082 --> 00:01:37.780
controlling its data and cost while it runs, and leaving no accidental exposure behind.
